Physical VS Abstract
Physical: describe the actual implementations in a computer's memory
Abstract: describes what operations are to be performed on the data.

////////// Definitions of specific Data Structure Types: \\\\\\\\\\\

1.Array

An array stores elements of the same type in contiguous memory
locations. Each element can be accessed using an index.

2. Linked List

A linked list consists of nodes, where each node stores data and a
reference to the next node. The nodes do not need to be stored
next to one another in memory.

3. Stack

A stack is a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) structure. The last element
added is the first one removed. Common operations are: push, pop, and peek.

4. Queue

A queue is a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) structure. The first element
added is the first one removed. Common operations are: enqueue

5. Sorted List

A sorted list maintains its elements in a specified order, such as
ascending or descending order. New elements are inserted in the
appropriate position to preserve that order

6. Map

A map stores data as key–value pairs. Each key is unique and is
used to access its associated value.

7. Tree

A tree is a hierarchical structure made up of nodes connected by
edges. It has a root node and may contain parent and child
nodes.

8.Graph

A graph consists of vertices (nodes) and edges (connections). It is
used to represent relationships such as road networks










